---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import BmrCalculator from '@components/islands/BmrCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';

const calc = getCalculator('health', 'bmr-calculator')!;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is BMR?',
    answer:
      'Basal Metabolic Rate is the number of calories your body burns at complete rest to maintain vital functions like breathing, circulation and cell production. It typically accounts for 60–70% of the calories you burn each day.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How is BMR calculated here?',
    answer:
      'By default it uses the Mifflin-St Jeor equation, widely regarded as one of the most accurate for healthy adults: 10 × weight(kg) + 6.25 × height(cm) − 5 × age, plus 5 for men or minus 161 for women. Under Settings you can switch to the Revised Harris-Benedict equation, or to Katch-McArdle, which works from lean body mass and so asks for your body fat percentage instead of using height and age.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which BMR formula should I use?',
    answer:
      'Mifflin-St Jeor is the sensible default for most people. Revised Harris-Benedict is the older equation and tends to read slightly higher. Katch-McArdle is the most accurate if — and only if — you have a reliable body fat measurement, because it works from lean mass rather than total weight; it is the one to pick if you are notably lean or notably muscular.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between BMR and TDEE?',
    answer:
      'BMR is your at-rest calorie burn. Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) multiplies BMR by an activity factor to include movement and exercise. Use the calorie calculator to get your TDEE.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does BMR decrease with age?',
    answer:
      'BMR tends to fall with age as muscle mass declines, since muscle burns more energy at rest than fat. Staying active and maintaining muscle helps keep your metabolic rate higher.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a normal BMR?',
    answer:
      'It varies widely with body size, sex, age and muscle mass, but many adults fall roughly in the 1,400–1,800 calories-per-day range. Larger and more muscular people have a higher BMR, which is why the figure is worth calculating for yourself rather than assuming an average.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How can I increase my BMR?',
    answer:
      'The most reliable lever is building and keeping muscle through resistance training, since muscle burns more at rest than fat. Staying generally active helps too. The effect is real but gradual — there is no shortcut to a dramatically higher metabolism.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Estimate your Basal Metabolic Rate — the calories your body burns at rest — then see what you would need each day at six activity levels."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-28' }}
>
  <BmrCalculator />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose <strong>US Units</strong> or <strong>Metric Units</strong>. Switching converts figures you have already typed rather than clearing them.</li>
      <li>Enter your <strong>age</strong> and select your <strong>gender</strong>.</li>
      <li>Enter your <strong>height</strong> and <strong>weight</strong>.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate BMR</strong> to see your BMR and what you would need each day at six activity levels.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>+ Settings</strong> holds the three things most people never change: which of the
      three published equations to use, a body fat percentage for the one that needs it, and
      whether to answer in Calories or kilojoules.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Other Units</strong> opens a pair of small converters above the calculator — one for
      height, one for weight. They are not a third unit system: use them when your scales read in
      stones or your tape reads in metres, then type the converted figure into the boxes. The
      calculator stays on whichever system you chose.
    </p>

    <h2>What BMR measures</h2>
    <p>
      Your Basal Metabolic Rate is the energy your body spends at complete rest just to keep you
      alive — breathing, circulating blood, regulating temperature and renewing cells. For most
      people it accounts for 60–70% of the calories burned in a day, which makes it the
      foundation for any nutrition plan. Everything you do on top of resting — walking, working,
      training — adds to it.
    </p>

    <h2>The three formulas</h2>
    <p>
      The calculator defaults to Mifflin-St Jeor, widely regarded as one of the most accurate for
      healthy adults. It works in metric units and differs only by a constant at the end for each
      sex:
    </p>
    <p><strong>Men: BMR = 10 × kg + 6.25 × cm − 5 × age + 5</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Women: BMR = 10 × kg + 6.25 × cm − 5 × age − 161</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>kg</strong> — body weight in kilograms</li>
      <li><strong>cm</strong> — height in centimetres</li>
      <li><strong>age</strong> — age in years</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Enter US figures and the calculator converts pounds and inches before applying the equation.</p>
    <p>
      Two alternatives sit under Settings. <strong>Revised Harris-Benedict</strong> (Roza and
      Shizgal, 1984) is the older equation, still widely quoted, and generally reads a little
      higher: 88.362 + 13.397 × kg + 4.799 × cm − 5.677 × age for men, and 447.593 + 9.247 × kg +
      3.098 × cm − 4.330 × age for women.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Katch-McArdle</strong> takes a different route: 370 + 21.6 × lean body mass in
      kilograms, where lean mass is your weight less your fat. It uses neither height nor age nor
      sex, because all three are only ever proxies for how much lean tissue you carry — so it is
      the most accurate of the three if you have a real body fat measurement, and the least
      useful if you are guessing at one. That is why it is the only equation that asks for a body
      fat percentage, and the only one that will refuse to answer without one.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Take a <strong>35-year-old man</strong> who is <strong>180 cm</strong> and
      <strong>80 kg</strong>. His BMR is 10 × 80 + 6.25 × 180 − 5 × 35 + 5 = 800 + 1,125 − 175 +
      5 = <strong>1,755 calories a day</strong>. That is what his body would burn if he spent the
      whole day at rest, before any movement is added.
    </p>

    <h2>From BMR to daily calories</h2>
    <p>
      To estimate the calories you actually need, multiply BMR by an activity factor. That
      product is your Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE), and it is what the table beside the
      calculator shows. Using the 1,755 BMR above:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Activity level</th><th>Factor</th><th>Daily calories</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Sedentary: little or no exercise</td><td>1.2</td><td>2,106</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Exercise 1-3 times/week</td><td>1.375</td><td>2,413</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Exercise 4-5 times/week</td><td>1.465</td><td>2,571</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Daily exercise or intense exercise 3-4 times/week</td><td>1.55</td><td>2,720</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Intense exercise 6-7 times/week</td><td>1.725</td><td>3,027</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Very intense exercise daily, or physical job</td><td>1.9</td><td>3,335</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      "Exercise" here means 15–30 minutes of elevated heart rate activity; "intense exercise"
      means 45–120 minutes; "very intense exercise" means two hours or more.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/health/calorie-calculator">calorie calculator</a> runs this step for you and
      adds weight-loss and weight-gain targets.
    </p>

    <h2>Why BMR varies from person to person</h2>
    <p>
      Body size is the biggest driver: a larger body has more tissue to maintain, so it burns
      more at rest. Sex and age matter too — the equation adds 5 for men and subtracts 161 for
      women, and takes off 5 for every year of age. Muscle is more metabolically active than fat,
      which is why two people of the same weight can have different rates.
    </p>

    <h2>Accuracy and limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>It is an estimate.</strong> Mifflin-St Jeor predicts the average for your stats; your true BMR can differ a little either way.</li>
      <li><strong>It does not read body composition.</strong> Mifflin-St Jeor and Harris-Benedict use total weight, not your muscle-to-fat split, so very muscular or very lean people may sit off the average — that is the case for switching to Katch-McArdle.</li>
      <li><strong>Healthy adults.</strong> Pregnancy, some medical conditions and the extremes of body size are not well captured by a general equation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For your full daily needs and goal targets, use the
      <a href="/health/calorie-calculator">calorie calculator</a>; to gauge body composition,
      see the <a href="/health/body-fat-calculator">body fat calculator</a>. This is educational
      information, not medical advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
